.TH FMT_ESCAPECHARNONPRINTABLE 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
fmt_escapecharnonprintable \- Escape non-printable characters

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/fmt.h>

.IB size_t
.IB fmt_escapecharnonprintable(char* dest, int c);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBfmt_escapecharnonprintable\fR function escapes non-printable characters. It takes a destination buffer and a character as arguments. The function escapes the character if it is non-printable, otherwise it copies the character unchanged.

The function takes the following parameters:
.IB char*
A pointer to the destination buffer.
.IB int
The character to escape.

The function returns the number of bytes written to the destination buffer.

The \fBfmt_escapecharnonprintable\fR function is intended to be used to escape non-printable characters for display, such as `\n`, `\r`, and `\t`.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBfmt_escapecharnonprintable\fR function to escape non-printable characters:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/fmt.h>

int main() {
    char dest[20];
    int c = '\n';

    size_t written = fmt_escapecharnonprintable(dest, c);

    printf("Written %d bytes: %s\n", written, dest);

    return 0;
}
.nef